# Tool sandbox

Register every domain tool in both `TOOLS` and `TOOL_FNS`. `SkillRegistry` slices those schemas by the manifest in `src/harness/skills/`. A manifest name missing from `TOOLS` fails at import. The loop sends `schemas()` for the loaded skills plus `load_skill` and `unload_skill`, not the full `TOOLS` list. `execute_tool` stays the raw sandbox; `SkillRegistry.execute` rejects a domain tool whose skill is not loaded.

Resolve every file path with `resolve_in_workspace` (`Path.resolve` plus `is_relative_to`). Reject empty paths and anything outside the workspace.

Return failures as `Error: ...` strings. `execute_tool` catches `ValueError` and `OSError`. `_tool_result` turns bad JSON into a tool error and still returns `(args_or_none, result)`. Integer arguments go through `_positive_int` / `_count_arg`, which reject zero, negatives, and booleans with `Error: <name> must be a positive integer`.

Cap model-facing text with `cap_result` (`MAX_TOOL_RESULT`). Every truncation marker must tell the model how to continue: pass a `hint` (a saved text file plus `read_file` with `offset`, the next page, a narrower pattern).

File tools: `read_file` pages with 1-based `offset` and `limit`, cuts a long result on a line boundary, and ends with the next `offset`. `read_file`, `delete_file`, `edit_file`, and `move_file` refuse directories. `write_file` and `append_file` create parents and report the byte count. `edit_file` requires `old_text` to match exactly once unless `replace_all`. `move_file` never overwrites. `list_files` and `search_files` skip `SKIP_DIRS`, cap output at `LIST_FILES_MAX` / `SEARCH_FILES_MAX`, and skip files that are not UTF-8 text.

`run_shell` stays `shell=False`, allowlisted env, `timeout` default `SHELL_TIMEOUT` capped at `SHELL_MAX_TIMEOUT`, optional `stdin`. Block `SHELL_META` and `DENIED_COMMANDS` (`rm`, `mv`, `chmod`, `sudo`). Reject `..` in argv parts. Check absolute args with `resolve_in_workspace`. `run_python` runs `sys.executable -I -c code` through the same `_run_process` with the same limits; it has the same trust level as `run_shell`.

Both commands go through `sandboxed_argv` in `sandbox.py`. On macOS the child calls `sandbox_init` before exec. The Seatbelt profile allows read and write only in the workspace, read and execute of the OS and Python runtime roots, and nothing else. `HOME` and `TMPDIR` (`workspace/.tmp`, skipped by `SKIP_DIRS`) point at the workspace. Network is denied. If the sandbox cannot be applied, including on any OS other than macOS, the command is not run.

HTTP: `validate_url` allows only `http` and `https`, with no userinfo. Resolve the host and reject any address that is not global, and do the same on every redirect. Disable proxies. All requests go through `_open_request` with a browser-like user agent; bodies are capped at `FETCH_MAX_BYTES`, requests at `FETCH_TIMEOUT`, and media types must be in `FETCH_TYPES` (HTML, plain text, Markdown, CSV, JSON, XML and feeds, PDF). `fetch_url` saves the body in the workspace (default `downloads/<host>-<hash><ext>`, extension from the media type) and returns status, content type, byte count, and path.

`parse_html` reads a saved workspace file and returns title, author, date, and text through `extract_page`: trafilatura article mode, then trafilatura with `favor_recall`, then a stdlib text fallback; `mode: full` goes straight to the fallback. It does not download. `read_url` is fetch plus extract in one call: it saves the raw file and a `.txt` copy, and its truncation hint names that text file. PDFs go through pypdf; other text types are returned as-is.

`web_search` POSTs the query to DuckDuckGo's HTML results page with optional `site:` and `df=` (`time` in `d`, `w`, `m`, `y`). Parse titles, snippets, and `uddg` redirect targets with the stdlib HTML parser. Drop a result whose URL is not public http(s). Default to 5 results and cap at `SEARCH_MAX_RESULTS`. Empty queries are `Error: query is required`. A page with nothing parseable is `Error: no results`. The result ends with `SEARCH_HINT` so the model reads pages instead of answering from snippets.

`read_feed`, `read_docx`, and any other XML go through `_parse_xml`, which refuses `DOCTYPE` and `ENTITY` declarations before calling ElementTree. `get_json` accepts only `JSON_TYPES`; `http_get_json`, `wikipedia_search`, and `wikipedia_summary` build on it.

`remember` / `recall` keep `## key` sections in `MEMORY_FILE`; `memory_keys` feeds the system prompt. `write_plan` / `update_plan` / `show_plan` keep `N. [ ]` lines in `PLAN_FILE`.

```python
# BAD: shell=True, or raising into the loop
subprocess.run(command, shell=True, cwd=workspace)

# GOOD: argv from prepare_shell, errors as strings
return f"Error: {exc}"
```
